+# UTC reference anchoring in Basic Station
+
+Wifx patch. Applies to LORIX OS gateways running Basic Station without a GNSS receiver.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Why this patch exists
+
+### The short version
+
+Basic Station publishes a `rxtime` field with every uplink it forwards to the LNS. On an
+unpatched gateway that value is wrong in two independent ways: it carries a fixed error of
+up to several seconds established when the process starts, and it drifts continuously
+afterwards. The gateway is the only party that can fix this. No LNS can repair a timestamp
+it did not produce.
+
+### The objection, answered
+
+*"`rxtime` is not meant to be used for timing anyway, so the LNS should not depend on it."*
+
+That is correct, and it does not change anything.
+
+`rxtime` is sampled when the JSON message is built, not when the frame is received, so it
+already carries the RX polling interval and the queueing delay. It is metadata. Basic
+Station provides `xtime` for correlating uplinks and downlinks, and `gpstime` when a GNSS
+is present. An LNS that uses `rxtime` as its primary timing reference is building on a
+field that was never specified to carry that weight.
+
+But two things are true at once. An LNS should not depend on `rxtime` for timing, **and**
+a gateway should not publish a value that is wrong by seconds and diverging without bound.
+The second is our responsibility and only ours. A customer opened a ticket because of it;
+the next customer, with a different LNS, would eventually do the same.
+
+### What was actually wrong
+
+**Defect 1: the UTC reference is captured once and never re-derived.**
+
+`rt_utcOffset` is read from the system clock in `rt_ini()`, at process start, and no code
+path ever reads the system clock again. On a gateway that starts Basic Station before the
+time daemon has converged, and that is the normal case on a 4G unit where the modem must
+attach before NTP can run, whatever the clock was wrong by at that instant becomes a
+permanent offset. Measured in the field on customer hardware: between 0.8 and 5 seconds
+depending on the boot.
+
+**Defect 2: without PPS, the reference is steered to the concentrator, not to NTP.**
+
+The only subsequent update to `rt_utcOffset` is a relative drift correction that aligns it
+to the SX1302 counter. That counter is driven by a TCXO, which is an excellent frequency
+source but has no notion of UTC and is two to three orders of magnitude worse than an
+NTP-disciplined host clock. The published timestamp therefore drifts at the TCXO's rate
+error, measured between 3 and 5 ppm across two gateways, while a perfectly healthy chrony
+running on the same machine is never consulted.
+
+---
+
+## 2. What the patch does
+
+A loop runs on its own timer, independent of the radio synchronisation loop and of the LNS
+session. Every `UTCANCHOR_INTV` it asks the kernel whether the system clock is disciplined,
+and if so compares the published UTC reference against it.
+
+- Small errors are corrected by a bounded amount per check.
+- Large errors are corrected in a single step, after confirmation.
+- If the kernel reports the clock as undisciplined, the loop does nothing and the previous
+  behaviour applies unchanged.
+
+Trust comes from `adjtimex()` in mode 0: the reference is used only when the call does not
+return `TIME_ERROR` and `STA_UNSYNC` is clear. There is no dependency on chrony, on its
+socket, or on any particular time daemon.
+
+### Where the loop is armed
+
+Once per process, from `startupMaster()`, and deliberately not from `ts_iniTimesync()`.
+
+`ts_iniTimesync()` runs before the fork in daemon mode, so the timer would also be queued in
+the supervisor, which reads no UTC reference yet would log under the same module tag and
+hand its step state to every worker it respawns. It also runs on every reconnect, which
+would restart the pending-step decision on an unstable link and drive the loop at the
+reconnect rate instead of `UTCANCHOR_INTV` - and the bound on the gradual correction assumes
+the configured cadence.
+
+No anchor state is tied to the LNS session: the deferral counter, the grace window, the step
+latch and the slew report timestamp all track the OS clock. `startupMaster()` still runs
+before the LNS connection is set up, so anchoring does not wait for the network.
+
+### Arbitration between the writers of `rt_utcOffset`
+
+Before the patch, four code paths wrote that variable with no ordering between them, and
+whichever ran last owned the value. The patch establishes a precedence:
+
+```
+  PPS logic                    highest, when a PPS signal is present
+  OS wall clock                this patch
+  router_config MuxTime        LNS-supplied, used when the OS clock is not trusted
+  MCU/SX130X drift steering    fallback when nothing better exists
+  rt_ini()                     initial value at process start
+```
+
+Each writer stands down while a higher one is available. Nothing is removed; the fallbacks
+remain in place exactly where they are needed.
+
+The `MuxTime` decision deserves a word, because it is a policy choice rather than a bug
+fix. `MuxTime` is a timestamp placed in a JSON message by the LNS and read on arrival, with
+no round-trip compensation and no filtering. Its error is the one-way network delay, which
+on a cellular link is tens to hundreds of milliseconds and is systematically biased late. A
+locally disciplined clock is better by two to three orders of magnitude and its error is
+zero-mean. On a gateway with no usable local clock, `MuxTime` still applies as before.
+
+---
+
+## 3. Measured results
+
+All figures from production hardware, not simulation.
+
+### Cold boot with a wrong clock
+
+An Ethernet gateway booted with its clock reading 2000-01-01. Basic Station started 47
+seconds before the time daemon converged, then:
+
+| | |
+|---|---|
+| Error at the moment trust was granted | 839 671 237 776 784 µs, about 26.6 years |
+| Corrected by | one step, immediately |
+| Residual afterwards | between -1 and +2 µs for the following 12 minutes |
+
+### Before and after on the same 4G gateway
+
+One log containing a restart, so the two regimes are directly comparable:
+
+| | Before | After |
+|---|---|---|
+| Slope of the published UTC | -4.2 ppm | -0.17 ppm |
+| Extrapolated over 7 days | -2.5 s | below 1 ms |
+
+Confirmed seven days later on the same gateway: the error was unchanged within 1 ms.
+
+### Robustness to a noisy radio loop
+
+On the 4G gateway the internal drift estimator produces large excursions, up to
+**+11 410 ppm**, most likely because modem activity stalls the SPI reads of the concentrator
+counter. Over the same period the published timestamp did not move by half a millisecond.
+That is the property the patch is for: the radio loop's noise no longer reaches the UTC
+output.
+
+### Coverage
+
+Every path through the loop exercised on production hardware.
+
+| Path | Ethernet gateway | 4G gateway |
+|---|---|---|
+| Clock untrusted, no action taken | boot with the clock at 2000-01-01 | 60 s after a manual `date -s`, and at boot |
+| Gradual correction | 22.9 ms absorbed in 110 s | 28 ms absorbed in 140 s |
+| Step, first of the process | 26.6 years, and separately 1.02 s | 2.87 s |
+| Step, deferred by `UTCANCHOR_STEP_CNT` | | several, `(1/3)` then `(2/3)` then step |
+| Second step inside the grace window | | twice, reported at WARNING |
+| Third step within the hour | | once, reported at ERROR |
+| Trust lost and regained | | six cycles in 40 minutes, one log line each, no flood |
+| Feature disabled by configuration | | `UTCANCHOR_ENABLE=0`, anchor never armed |
+| Steady state | ±2 µs over 12 minutes | 7 days within 1 ms |
+
+The only path never exercised is the release of the reference to the PPS logic, which
+requires PPS hardware.
+
+One behaviour worth knowing, discovered rather than designed: setting the clock manually
+with `date -s` makes the kernel raise `STA_UNSYNC`. The loop therefore refuses to act on a
+hand-stepped clock and waits for the time daemon to re-establish synchronisation. It held
+off for a full minute against a 2.87 s error before correcting it.
+
+Note also that the allowance for an immediate first step is keyed on whether a step has ever
+been taken in this process, not on elapsed time. A process that anchors, runs for hours
+without ever stepping, and then meets a large error will still step on the first check
+rather than after `UTCANCHOR_STEP_CNT` confirmations. Keying it on a once-per-process latch
+rather than on the anchored state also means a flapping trust bit cannot produce a train of
+unconfirmed steps.
+
+---
+
+## 4. Parameters
+
+Six runtime parameters, set in the `station_conf` section of `station.conf` or in the
+environment, both read before the anchor is armed, so switching a site over needs no
+rebuild. Three further values are compile-time constants because no deployment should need
+to change them.
+
+| Parameter | Default | When you would touch it |
+|---|---|---|
+| `UTCANCHOR_ENABLE` | `1` | Set to `0` to disable the feature entirely and restore the previous behaviour. The kill switch. See the warning below. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_INTV` | `10s` | Trade reactivity against cost. One `adjtimex()` and one `clock_gettime()` per interval. Rarely worth changing. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_STEP_THRES` | `200ms` | The boundary between a gradual correction and a single jump. Raise it if you would rather never jump; lower it if you want faster recovery from a large error. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_STEP_CNT` | `3` | Consecutive checks above the threshold before stepping. Raise it on a site whose time source is unreliable, so that no isolated bad reading can move the reference. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_SLEW_PPM` | `200` | Maximum correction rate. At the default, 2 ms per 10 s interval. Raise it if convergence after a disturbance is too slow. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_ESTERROR` | `0` | Refuse to anchor while the kernel's own error estimate exceeds this. `0` disables the check. See section 6 before enabling it. |
+
+**Warning on `UTCANCHOR_ENABLE`.** Setting it to `0` does not switch the UTC reference over
+to the LNS. It disables the anchor and lets `router_config MuxTime` write the reference
+again, which is only useful if your LNS actually sends that field. On an LNS that does not,
+setting `0` restores the pre-patch behaviour including its drift, with nothing in its place.
+
+### Compile-time constants
+
+| Constant | Value | Why it is not configurable |
+|---|---|---|
+| `UTCANCHOR_DEADBAND` | 1 ms | Three orders of magnitude below anything downstream can use. `rxtime` is already quantised by the RX polling interval. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_STEP1_CNT` | 1 | The first step of a process corrects an offset that is known to exist, at a moment the kernel has just declared the clock usable. Deferring it buys nothing. |
+| `UTCANCHOR_GRACE` | 5 min | Log severity policy only. Determines how long after startup an extra step is reported as a warning rather than an error. |
+
+---
+
+## 5. Reading the logs
+
+Module `SYN`. Everything below is visible at log level `VERBOSE` except the per-check
+diagnostic, which needs `DEBUG`.
+
+### Normal operation
+
+At startup, once per process, the effective configuration:
+
+```
+[SYN:INFO] UTC anchor: intv=10000ms deadband=1000us step=200000us cnt=1/3 grace=300000ms slew=200ppm esterror=0us
+```
+
+This line is the fastest way to confirm which build is running and what values it is using.
+If it appears more than once per process, something is wrong.
+
+When the clock becomes usable:
+
+```
+[SYN:INFO] UTC anchor: OS clock trusted - taking over the UTC reference (error -1us, maxerror 26724us)
+```
+
+And then, on a healthy gateway, **nothing at all**. Seven days of production running
+produced no further line. Silence is the expected steady state; the loop checks and finds
+nothing to correct.
+
+At `DEBUG` level, one line per interval, useful for diagnosis:
+
+```
+[SYN:DEBU] UTC anchor: trusted=1 anchored=1 err=0us maxerror=21000us esterror=22us
+```
+
+### Lines that mean something happened
+
+| Line | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `error Nus beyond slew range (i/n) - deferring` | An error above the step threshold awaiting confirmation. Expected before any step other than the first of the process. |
+| `stepping UTC reference by Nus - error beyond slew range` | Expected once per boot when the clock was wrong at process start. |
+| `stepping ... N steps within the hour, time source flapping or kernel timekeeping faulty` | An error. A repeated step in steady state is a fault, not a correction. |
+| `stepping ... step N in startup grace, time daemon likely trusted before converged` | The time daemon declared the clock usable before it had converged. Harmless, but it is the evidence for enabling `UTCANCHOR_ESTERROR`. |
+| `slewing UTC reference by Nus` | A gradual correction is in progress. Rate-limited to `TIMESYNC_REPORTS`, 5 minutes by default. |
+| `OS clock no longer trusted ... reverting to MCU/SX130X drift steering` | NTP has been unavailable long enough for the kernel to give up. The gateway falls back to the previous behaviour. |
+| `PPS acquired - releasing the UTC reference to the PPS logic` | A PPS signal appeared. The anchor stands down and the PPS logic governs alone. |
+| `disabled by UTCANCHOR_ENABLE=0` | The feature is off by configuration. |
+
+### One thing this patch is not responsible for
+
+For the first seconds or minutes after the process starts you may see a burst of
+
+```
+[SYN:WARN] Repeated excessive clock drifts between MCU/SX130X#0 (N retries): ...ppm (threshold 100.0ppm)
+```
+
+with values in the hundreds or thousands of ppm, decaying steadily. This is a **separate,
+pre-existing defect** in the radio synchronisation loop, unrelated to the UTC anchor and
+present long before this patch. It is tracked on its own.
+
+It matters here only because the two are easy to confuse. Since the anchor stands the drift
+steering down while the OS clock is trusted, none of that noise reaches the published
+`rxtime`. If you see this burst alongside a stable `rxtime`, both are behaving as expected.
+
+---
+
+## 6. Behaviour when NTP is unavailable
+
+**Never available since boot.** `STA_UNSYNC` stays set, the loop never takes over, and the
+concentrator drift steering runs exactly as it did before this patch. The cost is one
+`adjtimex()` per interval. This is deliberate: a gateway with no time source must not behave
+differently because of this feature.
+
+**Lost while running.** The kernel keeps applying the last frequency correction to both the
+monotonic and the wall clock, so the measured error stays near zero and the loop takes no
+action and logs nothing. The published UTC drifts at the residual of the discipline, well
+under 1 ppm, rather than at the TCXO's several ppm.
+
+The kernel's own error estimate grows at 500 µs per second of unsynchronised operation, and
+it declares the clock unusable at 16 seconds. That is roughly **8.9 hours** without any NTP
+update before the fallback of the previous paragraph is triggered.
+
+### About `UTCANCHOR_ESTERROR`
+
+The kernel exposes two error estimates. Both are logged; only one is offered as a guard, and
+even that one is a coarse gate rather than a precision instrument.
+
+`maxerror` is a sawtooth: it resets at each NTP update and grows at 500 µs per second in
+between. Measured between 2.5 ms and 30 ms across a 129 second polling interval, which means
+any useful threshold would have to sit above 35 ms and would still be dominated by the
+sawtooth. It is logged for diagnosis and is not offered as a guard.
+
+`esterror` is the one you can act on, with two important limits.
+
+**It does not predict the real error.** Six measurements taken at the moment trust was
+granted, some during a deliberate sequence of clock disturbances:
+
+| Reported `esterror` | Real error | Deviation |
+|---|---|---|
+| 30.7 ms | 22.9 ms | over by 34 % |
+| 840 ms | 1022 ms | under by 18 % |
+| 3.2 ms | 28.1 ms | under by 89 % |
+| 1654 ms | 2871 ms | under by 42 % |
+| 1393 ms | 2141 ms | under by 35 % |
+| 565 ms | 153 ms | **over by 268 %** |
+
+The spread runs from underestimating by a factor of nine to overestimating by a factor of
+three and a half. It reliably catches gross errors, at the second level, and lets fine ones
+through, at the tens of milliseconds level. Treat it as a gate against anchoring on a badly
+converged clock, never as a bound on the resulting accuracy.
+
+**Its steady-state value depends entirely on the network path.** Measured in steady state:
+10 to 144 µs on an Ethernet gateway, 3.2 to 11.4 ms on a 4G gateway. A factor of a hundred.
+A threshold sized from an Ethernet unit would permanently block anchoring on a cellular one.
+
+If you enable it, capture a few minutes at `DEBUG` on the noisiest link in your fleet, take
+the highest steady-state value you see, and leave an order of magnitude of margin above it.
+
+---
+
+## 7. What this patch does not do
+
+**It does not enable Class B.** Beaconing requires GPS time, which requires a PPS signal.
+Without one, `ppsOffset` is never established, the time synchronisation handshake with the
+LNS never starts, and `gpstime` stays unavailable. This is unchanged.
+
+**It does not enable GPS-time scheduled downlinks**, including multicast FUOTA sessions,
+for the same reason.
+
+**It does not make the timestamp better than NTP.** The published UTC is now as good as the
+gateway's time synchronisation, which is a few milliseconds on a healthy link. It is not a
+substitute for a GNSS receiver.
+
+**It does not change transmit timing.** There is no conversion from UTC back to internal
+time anywhere in Basic Station; every transmission is scheduled from the concentrator
+counter. The UTC reference is a write-only output. A step in the reference cannot move a
+transmission by one microsecond.
+
+---
+
+## 8. Two things to expect after upgrading
+
+**One backwards jump per boot.** Shortly after each boot, once the time daemon has
+converged, the gateway corrects its reference. If the clock was wrong at process start,
+`rxtime` will jump backwards once, by up to a few seconds, roughly 10 to 40 seconds after
+the process starts. Uplinks received before that correction carry the old, wrong timestamp.
+
+This happens once per reboot and it replaces an error that was previously permanent. But an
+LNS that orders or deduplicates on `rxtime` may see it. Tell your integrators before they
+discover it.
+
+**The published UTC is not strictly monotonic.** A gradual correction is a single bounded
+assignment, not a rate: it can move the reference by up to
+`UTCANCHOR_SLEW_PPM * UTCANCHOR_INTV` at once, 2 ms at the defaults. Two timestamps taken
+less than that apart and straddling a correction can come back inverted. Beyond one check
+interval any two samples are strictly ordered, since the accumulated correction then grows
+at `UTCANCHOR_SLEW_PPM`, 0.02 % of the elapsed time. Steps are exempt by construction, which
+is what the confirmation count and the log lines are for.
+
+Making the slew a true rate would turn the UTC reference into a two-term state that all of
+its writers would have to maintain consistently, to remove a discontinuity that only occurs
+while converging out of a step.
\ No newline at end of file
diff --git a/src-linux/sys_linux.c b/src-linux/sys_linux.c
index 2bbb7e5..8895a52 100644
--- a/src-linux/sys_linux.c
+++ b/src-linux/sys_linux.c
@@ -37,6 +37,7 @@
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/stat.h>
 #include <sys/wait.h>
+#include <sys/timex.h>
 
 #include "argp2.h"
 #include "s2conf.h"
@@ -468,6 +469,23 @@ sL_t sys_utc () {
 }
 
 
+// adjtimex mode 0 is a privilege-free query of the kernel NTP discipline state.
+int sys_utcTrusted (sL_t* maxerror, sL_t* esterror) {
+    struct timex tx;
+    memset(&tx, 0, sizeof(tx));
+    if( maxerror ) *maxerror = 0;
+    if( esterror ) *esterror = 0;
+    int state = adjtimex(&tx);
+    if( state == -1 ) {
+        LOG(MOD_SYS|DEBUG, "adjtimex(2) failed: %s", strerror(errno));
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if( maxerror ) *maxerror = tx.maxerror;
+    if( esterror ) *esterror = tx.esterror;
+    return state != TIME_ERROR && (tx.status & STA_UNSYNC) == 0;
+}
+
+
 str_t sys_version () {
     return readFileAsString("version", ".txt", &versionTxt);
 }
@@ -1045,6 +1063,7 @@ static void startupMaster (tmr_t* tmr) {
     // If there is an update pending - run it
     sys_runUpdate();
     ral_ini();
+    ts_startUtcAnchor();
     atexit(leds_off);
     // Wait until slaves are up
     //startupMaster2(tmr);
